/** Label and roster rewrites. Facts from the site and clubcopy / artist Bandcamp pages. */
module.exports = {
  "lt-drifta-on-club-copy": {
    format: "Feature",
    kicker: "Feature",
    dek: "Five Club Copy titles, three different genre lines, and a Cumberland address: what the pages actually say about L.T. Drifta.",
    paras: [
      `A signing note is usually a mood. This one has to be a contents page, because the mood was doing too much work. L.T. Drifta is the producer Cumberland, British Columbia, on the Club Copy artist page, with nine titles listed and a one-line brief: “Exploring the soulful side of jungle and hip-hop.” The same site’s roster shortens the place to Cumberland, BC, tags the music “Jungle / Breaks,” and changes the sentence to “the soulful side of the electronic underground.” The catalogue data says something else again: “Instrumental hip-hop & soulful breaks,” and “the soulful side of electronic breakbeats.” Three public descriptions, one name. That is not a branding problem to be smoothed out. It is the most accurate thing the label has published about him.`,
      `The nine titles, in the order the artist page prints them, are <em>Lavery - Shaolin Style (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em> (CC028, a single, 24 October 2026), <em>The Chameleon - Links (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em> (CC024, a single, 23 October 2026), <em>Omni Trio - Renegade Snares (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em> (CC023, a single, 23 October 2026), <em>Nia Archives - Get Me Down (Molly's Hazy Jungle Edit)</em> (CC020, a single, 19 October 2026), <em>Three 4 Zone</em> (CC014, a single), <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em> (CC008, an EP, VCR026D3), <em>Summer Madness</em> (CC006, an album, also marked VCR026D2), <em>Classic Jazz</em> (CC003, an album, VCR026D1), and <em>Champion Sound</em> (CC001, a single). The page says “Nine titles on Club Copy” and stops. It does not say who plays on them, whether the name is one person, or what the second codes mean. Those are questions for a conversation the desk is not going to invent. What a reader can do, without anyone’s permission, is read the nine release pages against the Bandcamp sleeves and notice what stays constant.`,
      `The constant is not a tempo. <em>Champion Sound</em> is filed on its own page as instrumental hip-hop. <em>Classic Jazz</em> is filed as jazz. <em>Summer Madness</em>, <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em>, and <em>Three 4 Zone</em> are filed as electronic. Jungle, the word on the artist page and the roster, does not appear as the genre line on any of the five. A curious listener who does not spend their weekends inside genre arguments can take that at face value: the label is using “jungle” as a description of where the ears go, and using the release pages for what each object actually is. Hip-hop, jazz, and electronic music share a 20th-century habit of building new records out of older ones. The pages never say these five do that. They do keep naming tracks after music that already has a history. That pattern is legible. It does not require a myth.`,
      `The dates follow the numbers, and the tidy version is the one to memorize. Bandcamp, which is the sleeve a buyer outside this website actually sees, dates <em>Classic Jazz</em> to 4 October 2025, <em>Summer Madness</em> to 14 November 2025, <em>Champion Sound</em> to 9 July 2024, and <em>Three 4 Zone</em> to 18 October 2026. <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em> is dated 10 December 2025 on clubcopy.ca and is not on the Bandcamp music list; the album URL in the label’s data returns a missing page. So the catalogue number CC001 is the same thing as “the record that reached the public first.” <em>Classic Jazz</em> and <em>Summer Madness</em> came after the record that opens the numbering. A label that wants to be read in five years has to leave that sequence visible. Retroactive origin stories are how small catalogues start lying to themselves.`,
      `There is a second public half of the work, and it is not for sale. The mixes page on this site lists forty uploads from the L.T. Drifta SoundCloud. The titles run in series: <em>Deep in Tha Jungle</em> (with years in the names: 1993–96, 1994 hardcore, 1996 atmosphere), <em>Liquid Love</em>, <em>Loft Music</em>, <em>Café Après</em>, a shoegaze and dreampop run called <em>blissed out</em>, <em>Club Copy Radio</em>, and <em>NIGHTSHIFT</em>, which shares a name with this magazine. The records are the part with a price. The mixes are the part that shows the listening. Treating only the nine titles as the artist would be like reviewing a novelist from the bookshop and ignoring the library card. This note is the index those records were missing when the desk first filed him, on 3 May 2024, in a paragraph that talked about temperature and said nothing about a catalogue number.`,
      `The critical point is modest and worth keeping. L.T. Drifta is not, on the evidence of the pages, a jungle producer who sometimes makes beats, or a beatmaker who sometimes says jungle. He is a Cumberland producer whose label has refused to pick one sentence, and whose public work splits cleanly into priced records and a much larger body of mixes. That split is more useful than a genre. It tells you where to start: the nine titles if you want the objects, the mixes if you want the map, and the disagreement between the sentences if you want to know how a small label actually describes someone it works with. The 2024 note mistook atmosphere for a biography. The biography, so far, is the discography.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/lt-drifta`, `Artist page`],
      [`/tapes`, `Mixtapes`]
    ]
  },
  "champion-sound": {
    format: "Release",
    kicker: "Release",
    dek: "CC001 is one instrumental track, 4:53, dated 9 July 2024 on Bandcamp. The catalogue number and the public date agree.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Champion Sound</em> is the first number in the Club Copy catalogue and, on the public record, the first of L.T. Drifta’s records to appear. The release page on this site describes a single of 4:53, priced at $1.50 CAD, and files it as instrumental hip-hop. The three sentences of copy are the whole brief: “The room after the bridge. Crate heat. Sessions that don’t apologize for staying quiet.” Produced for Club Copy and Copy House Publishing. That is what the label was willing to say in its own voice. It is also, read cold, a description of a beat record rather than a club record. No DJ tool, no mix, no vocal, no invitation to a room. A single that asks to be left on.`,
      `The date is one plain fact. The Club Copy page prints 9 July 2024. Bandcamp, at ltdrifta.bandcamp.com/track/champion-sound, prints 9 July 2024, and the label’s own catalogue data agrees with Bandcamp. The sleeve a buyer can check without our permission says the same. So CC001 reached the public on 9 July 2024, before <em>Classic Jazz</em> (4 October) and <em>Summer Madness</em> (14 November), and before <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em> on the clubcopy.ca date for that EP. The number says “opening statement.” The calendar says the opening statement arrived before the standards album and the short-form album. Both facts can be true. Neither of them is a romance about origins.`,
      `What the number does mean is a choice of first principles. Independent electronic labels often open on a dance track, because a dance track explains the night they want to be invited to. Club Copy opened its numbering on a four-minute-and-fifty-three-second instrumental and called it hip-hop on the page that sells it. The artist page, elsewhere, says jungle. The roster says jungle and breaks. None of those words is on this release. A listener who does not DJ can hear the implication without a tutorial: the label’s first object is a beat record, and the dance music comes in other catalogue numbers, under other names. Forsberg’s house, Riscape’s piano, Molly Haze’s garage line on the roster — those are different arguments. <em>Champion Sound</em> is the argument that a quiet session counts as a release.`,
      `“Crate heat” is doing specific work in that copy, and it is worth taking seriously rather than as scenery. In hip-hop and in the break-based music that became jungle, a crate is not a mood. It is the pile of older records a producer plays, chops, and answers. The page does not name a sample, and this piece will not invent one. What it will say is that the label chose a phrase from digging culture for its first number, then paired it with “sessions that don’t apologize for staying quiet.” Volume is not the proof. Attention is. That is a very old idea in instrumental hip-hop — the beat as a place you stay, not a drop you wait for — and it is a useful one for a label whose about page says “Music we want to keep.” Keeping implies a time longer than a peak-hour play.`,
      `The title itself is a common phrase in this music. “Champion sound” has been a bit of dancehall and reggae language for decades, a way of praising a system or a tune, and it has titled more than one famous record. The Club Copy page does not say this single refers to any of them. The useful reading is local: a small Pacific Northwest label, Bandcamp address Cumberland, British Columbia, giving its first catalogue number a name that sounds like a standard and then describing a quiet room. The grandeur is in the name. The copy walks it back. That tension is the record’s public personality. If a later history of the label needs one sentence for CC001, it is this: they began by numbering a beat, and the number and the date agree.`,
      `Heard against the other four L.T. Drifta titles, <em>Champion Sound</em> is the short credo. <em>Classic Jazz</em> shows the standards. <em>Summer Madness</em> shows the short-form album. <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em> shows a two-track EP the Bandcamp list does not currently carry. <em>Three 4 Zone</em>, at 6:41 and dated 18 October 2026, is longer than this single and later than everything else. CC001 remains the sentence the label put at the front of the binder. Quiet, instrumental, one track, a bridge already crossed. The afters are not the subject. The session is.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/champion-sound`, `Champion Sound`],
      [`/artists/lt-drifta`, `L.T. Drifta`],
    ]
  },
  "summer-madness": {
    format: "Release",
    kicker: "Release",
    dek: "Eight short electronic tracks, released 14 November 2025. The Bandcamp sleeve times Summer Madness at 20:35.",
    paras: [
      `<em>Summer Madness</em> is L.T. Drifta’s CC006, an eight-track album on Club Copy, digital, $9 CAD, also marked VCR026D2 on the artist page. The label’s description is the warmest sentence it has written about him: “Warm room. Nobody wants to leave. Vancouver late hours with jazz and soul in the flips.” Released 14 November 2025. Produced for Club Copy and Copy House Publishing. The genre line on the page is electronic, not jungle and not jazz, even though jazz is the word the copy reaches for. That mismatch is the album’s actual subject.`,
      `The running order, from the Bandcamp sleeve at ltdrifta.bandcamp.com/album/summer-madness, is “Space and Time” (2:07), “Go!” (3:37), “Summer Madness” (3:20), “Too Far” (2:55), “Nu Saigon” (1:00), “You’re My” (2:51), “So What” (1:43), and “Mood Indigo” (3:02). Added up, that is 20:35. The Club Copy page prints 20:41 and times a few tracks a second longer. The difference is small and the principle is not: when the two sleeves disagree, this piece follows Bandcamp, because that is the public object with the artist’s name on the account. Nothing on the album, by either count, runs to four minutes. “Nu Saigon” is exactly a minute. An album that brief is a decision about attention. It assumes you will notice a short track, and that you do not need a ten-minute version to believe the idea was finished.`,
      `A minute-long piece inside an album asks a different question than a minute-long sketch on a beat tape. On a tape, the short track is often an apology or a loop that escaped. Sequenced as track five of eight, between “Too Far” and “You’re My,” “Nu Saigon” is part of the form. The album is built like a set of postcards rather than a novel: each title gets a scene, and the scene ends. Listeners who came up on long club sides sometimes hear brevity as a lack of commitment. The other reading is available, and it fits the rest of his Club Copy run. <em>Classic Jazz</em> also keeps several pieces short. <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em> follows a 3:55 title track with “Hold U” at 1:11. The long single, <em>Three 4 Zone</em>, comes later. In November 2025 the method was compression.`,
      `The jazz and soul the cover copy mentions show up in the titles, and the titles are as far as a responsible review can go. “Summer Madness” is the name of a 1974 Kool &amp; the Gang instrumental, one of those records that circulated for decades as a mood rather than as a song with a plot. “So What” is the opening track of Miles Davis’s <em>Kind of Blue</em>. “Mood Indigo” is Duke Ellington’s. Whether these eight tracks use those recordings, replay them, or only borrow the names is not stated on the page or on Bandcamp. This magazine will not guess. What it will say is that naming an electronic album’s tracks after a funk instrumental, a modal-jazz standard, and an Ellington ballad, in a month when the same producer had just released <em>Classic Jazz</em>, is a pattern. The pattern is the canon as a set of titles a beatmaker keeps in reach. Homage, source, and coincidence are three different acts. The sleeve does not choose among them. The repetition across two albums makes “coincidence” the least satisfying of the three, without proving either of the others.`,
      `“Vancouver late hours” is the one place-name in the copy, and it is worth reading against the biography. L.T. Drifta’s artist page and the label’s Bandcamp both put him in Cumberland, a town on the east coast of Vancouver Island. Vancouver, in the album text, is not offered as his address. It is offered as a time of night: late, warm, nobody leaving. A non-DJ can follow that image without a scene report. It is the hour after the plans, when the music in the room is supposed to behave like weather. The critical risk in the sentence is that “warm” and “soul” can become a compliment that replaces listening. Eight tracks in twenty-odd minutes do not give a writer much camouflage. Either the sequence holds — the dash of “Go!,” the full stop of “Nu Saigon,” the Ellington title last — or the warmth is only advertising.`,
      `Set beside CC001, this is the record on which the label stops describing a single session and tries to describe an evening. <em>Champion Sound</em> is one room, quiet on purpose. <em>Summer Madness</em> is a run of rooms, all of them short, with the standards leaning in at the edges and never introduced. That is a more ambitious object than the copy’s coziness suggests. Cozy is easy to sell and hard to finish eight times without a track that overstays. On the timings, nothing overstays. The album’s claim is that a late hour can be edited, and that jazz in the titles is enough jazz to declare. The second claim is the one a listener should test. The first is already on the sleeve.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/summer-madness`, `Summer Madness`],
      [`/artists/lt-drifta`, `L.T. Drifta`],
    ]
  },
  "inlet-knight-on-club-copy": {
    format: "Feature",
    kicker: "Feature",
    dek: "Forsberg's Bandcamp says Cumberland. The label page says Vancouver. The records are the part that does not depend on the line.",
    paras: [
      `The August 2024 note that announced Forsberg was a temperature check with a catalogue problem: it named one record and a city, and it treated the signing as a feeling. Feelings date badly. Sleeves do not. Two years on, the public record is large enough to read, and one fact in it has to be settled before the music can be.`,
      `Bandcamp, at forsberg.bandcamp.com, describes the project in a line: “House music from Cumberland, BC.” The Club Copy artist page and the roster say Vancouver. The artist-page sentence is “House out of Vancouver — locked drums, long loops, and records finished when they leave.” The roster tag is “Lo-fi House,” with the bio “Sample-driven lo-fi house from the Pacific Northwest,” and the latest release flagged there is <em>Freed From (Nanana)</em>, CC029. The catalogue data repeats Vancouver. Where a biography disagrees with itself, this piece follows the Bandcamp account the artist’s page actually sells from. Forsberg is house from Cumberland. The label’s own pages have not caught up. That is a correction, not a scandal. It matters because a magazine that prints the wrong town for its own artist is not documenting a scene. It is misfiling one.`,
      `The Club Copy titles, as the site lists them, are <em>Freed From (Nanana)</em> (CC029, 24 October 2026), <em>w h a t ' s l u v</em> (CC027, 24 October 2026), <em>l i f e t i m e</em> (CC026, 24 October 2026), <em>call anytime</em> (CC025, 23 October 2026), <em>The Avalanches - I Give Everything I Have 2 U (ft. Loaded Honey) (Forsberg Refix)</em> (CC022, 23 October 2026), <em>Safe As</em> (CC018, 20 October 2026), <em>Deeper</em> (CC017, 19 October 2026), <em>Outta This Mess</em> (CC016, 18 October 2026), <em>Rose Rouge - St Germain (Molly's Hazy Edit) Jorja Smith Version</em> (CC015, 18 October 2026), <em>Pitch Black</em> (CC013, 18 October 2026, three tracks: “Neighbourhood Spy,” “Primo,” “Pitch Black”), <em>Try To Love Again</em> (CC012, 18 October 2026), <em>Run It Back</em> (CC011, 18 October 2026), <em>Please Wait</em> (CC010, 17 October 2026), and <em>j'adore</em> (CC007, 10 August 2026). <em>j'adore</em> is the one with a production credit on the page: CJ Archer, who also writes this zine and who the about page identifies as Charlie Archer, founder of the label. A review of that single has to carry the credit in the open. Independence is not improved by leaving a producer’s name off a sleeve when the sleeve printed it. Bandcamp hosts <em>j'adore</em> at forsberg.bandcamp.com/album/jadore — two tracks, “don't cry” (3:27) and “j'adore” (4:51) — not at the older rainiershouse address stored in the catalogue data, which does not resolve.`,
      `The Bandcamp music list and the Club Copy artist page now name the same Forsberg records, including <em>Deeper</em>, <em>Outta This Mess</em>, <em>Safe As</em>, and the Rose Rouge edit. The edit still raises rights questions this issue is not equipped to settle. What the label page does claim is a house practice: locked drums, long loops, records finished when they leave. “Lo-fi” on the roster and “locked drums, long loops” on the artist page are not identical aesthetics. Lo-fi usually means a tolerance for dust, hiss, and the sound of a small room. Locked drums usually mean the opposite pressure: a pattern that does not wobble. A producer can do both — dusty source, strict time — and a lot of contemporary house does. The pages simply have not decided which sentence is the face. Until they do, the honest introduction is to quote both and then point at the records.`,
      `<em>Pitch Black</em> is the useful test of “finished when they leave.” It is billed as an album and it holds three tracks, digital, $4 CAD, released the same day as two singles, <em>Run It Back</em> and <em>Try To Love Again</em>, and a day after <em>Please Wait</em>. Four Club Copy titles dated 17–18 October 2026 was a burst, not a drip. The shelf kept going after that count: <em>Outta This Mess</em> and the Rose Rouge edit on the 18th, <em>Deeper</em> on the 19th, <em>Safe As</em> on the 20th, <em>The Avalanches - I Give Everything I Have 2 U (ft. Loaded Honey) (Forsberg Refix)</em> and <em>call anytime</em> on the 23rd, and <em>l i f e t i m e</em>, <em>w h a t ' s l u v</em>, and <em>Freed From (Nanana)</em> on the 24th, each one track at $1.50. A burst can mean a drawer being emptied in public, which would fit “finished when they leave” perfectly: the record is done when it is no longer being protected. It can also mean a catalogue trying to look busy. The pages do not say which. They do say each of the October singles is one track at $1.50, and that <em>Pitch Black</em> is the one grouped as an album. Grouping is an editorial act. Three tracks under one title ask to be heard as a set. One track at a single’s price asks to be heard as a dispatch. Forsberg’s Club Copy story, read this way, moves from a two-track single in August to a small pile in October. The August record is the one with another producer’s name on it. The October records are billed only as Forsberg’s.`,
      `Cumberland, if the Bandcamp line holds, puts Forsberg in the same town the label gives L.T. Drifta and Molly Haze, and the same town Club Copy prints on its own Bandcamp. Vancouver remains on the marketing pages. The music does not need the dispute resolved in order to be heard, but a zine does, because place is part of the claim the label makes about itself: independent electronic music from the Pacific Northwest, founded, on the about page’s data, on 12 February 2024. A house producer in that picture is not an idea of a city. He is a name, a town, a credit, and a short run of records with drums the label calls locked and loops it calls long. Start with <em>j'adore</em> if you want the credit visible. Start with <em>Pitch Black</em> if you want the latest grouping. Start with <em>Freed From (Nanana)</em> if you want the newest single. Ignore any note, including the one this desk printed in 2024, that offers you a mood instead of a title.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/forsberg`, `Forsberg`],
      [`/safe-as`, `Safe As`],
      [`/j-adore`, `j'adore`],
      [`/pitch-black`, `Pitch Black`]
    ]
  },
  "j-adore": {
    format: "Release",
    kicker: "Release",
    dek: "Two Forsberg tracks, 10 August 2026. Bandcamp times them 3:27 and 4:51. The sleeve credits CJ Archer as producer.",
    paras: [
      `<em>j'adore</em> is CC007, the first Forsberg title on the Club Copy list, released 10 August 2026. Two tracks. On the clubcopy.ca page they are “don't cry” and “j'adore,” and the production credit is CJ Archer. On Bandcamp, at forsberg.bandcamp.com/album/jadore, the same two titles are timed 3:27 and 4:51, and the release date matches. Digital on the label’s page is $8 CAD. The genre line is house. That is the object. Everything else the August 2026 note said about cars, violet light, and a passenger was a listening diary. Diaries are allowed. They are not the sleeve.`,
      `The credit is the part a magazine published by this label has to put in the first paragraph. CJ Archer is the byline on almost every Night Shift piece, the founder named on the about page as Charlie Archer, and the producer named on this single. A reader who wants a review from outside the house will not find it here. What they can find is a description that does not pretend the credit is a footnote. <em>j'adore</em> is a Forsberg record produced by the person who also runs the imprint. Small labels have worked this way for as long as small labels have existed. The ethical requirement is not a different byline invented for the occasion. It is that the sentence stay visible when the record is discussed. Locked drums and long loops, the phrase on Forsberg’s artist page, arrive on this particular single through that collaboration. Whose hands did which part is not on the page. “Produced by CJ Archer” is.`,
      `Two tracks and under nine minutes, on the Bandcamp timings, is a small frame for a debut on a label. It suits the artist-page line “records finished when they leave.” A finished house track, in the tradition this music actually comes from — Chicago and New York in the 1980s, the long DJ mixes that taught the form, the edits that circulated on acetate and then on cheap digital files — does not have to be long to be a record. It does have to be done. “don't cry” at 3:27 is the length of a song. “j'adore” at 4:51 is the length of a slightly extended song. Neither is a ten-minute DJ tool, and the page does not claim a tool. It claims a single. Listeners who only trust house when it announces itself with a long intro will find this scale abrupt. The other tradition, the song that happens to be on a dance rhythm, is just as old. Riscape’s <em>You Are (Love)</em>, on the same label a month later, sits in that second tradition openly, with a piano player’s name in the credits. <em>j'adore</em> sits there more quietly: two titles, one of them a French endearment, neither of them explained.`,
      `Place, again, should be the Bandcamp place. “House music from Cumberland, BC” is the line on Forsberg’s Bandcamp. The Club Copy page says Vancouver. This review will not split the difference. Cumberland is a town on Vancouver Island. Vancouver is the larger city across the water. They produce different sentences about what “local” means, and the label has been printing the city while the artist account prints the town. For a single released in August 2026, the practical fact is smaller than the geography. A two-track house record came out, with a named producer, at a price, on a Pacific Northwest imprint whose Bandcamp also says Cumberland. The night the old note invented — windows down, a slow drive — is not on the sleeve. The sleeve is enough.`,
      `Heard as the start of a run rather than as a mood, <em>j'adore</em> is the prototype for the October titles. <em>Please Wait</em>, <em>Run It Back</em>, and <em>Try To Love Again</em> are each one track. <em>Pitch Black</em> gathers three. None of those later pages repeats the CJ Archer production credit. So the collaboration is, on the public record, specific to CC007. That makes the single more interesting, not less. It is the moment the label’s writer is also, officially, the producer of a roster record, and the moment Forsberg’s name enters the numbering. Later records will show what the name does without that credit printed. This one shows the credit. Play it as a song that ends, twice. The catalogue gets longer after that. The credit, so far, does not.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/j-adore`, `Listen to j'adore`],
      [`/artists/forsberg`, `Forsberg`],
      [`/news/inlet-knight-on-club-copy`, `We Signed Forsberg`]
    ]
  },
  "please": {
    format: "Release",
    kicker: "Release",
    dek: "Riscape's CC004: four house tracks, 15:02, released 18 September 2026. Bandcamp titles the last one Days Like This.",
    paras: [
      `<em>You Are (Love)</em> is CC004, Riscape’s first Club Copy record, an EP of four tracks running 15:02. The label’s page prices the digital edition at $9 CAD and says the cassette is sold out. It calls the cassette the first Club Copy cassette. Released 18 September 2026, the date on both the site and Bandcamp. The tracks, in order, are “You Are (Love)” (3:03), “Make It Right” (3:23), “Action” (4:15), and — on Bandcamp — “Days Like This” (4:21). The Club Copy page prints the last title as “Day Like This.” One letter, and the sort of letter that makes two databases disagree forever. The public sleeve at riscape.bandcamp.com/album/you-are-love says “Days.” This piece follows that.`,
      `The Bandcamp note is the artist’s, and it is the only statement from him this review needs. Ioannis Rigas writes that he wanted something “vintage but contemporary,” a groove that paid homage to “the classic house structures and sounds of our time,” and he signs the work as written by Ioannis Rigas and produced by Riscape for Club Copy and Copy House Publishing. That is a clearer credit than most dance EPs bother with. Writer and producer are separated. The homage is declared rather than implied by a filter. Vintage-but-contemporary is a crowded promise in 2020s house — a lot of records mean “a Rhodes and a shaker.” The value of the sentence is that it tells a non-specialist what the EP thinks it is doing: song form, classic structures, color, respect. Whether it earns the respect is a listening question. The intention is no longer a guess.`,
      `Riscape’s artist page supplies the biography the EP sits inside. Athens. Piano at Chalandri’s National Conservatory, then production study at SAE Athens and Grey Studios. Releases from 2020. A run of melodic house on the Dutch label Mystery Freedom from 2021, including EPs called <em>Unity</em> and <em>Two x S</em>. The page also says that in 2026 his remix of “Synth Magic,” by Alphed Le Cornett and JARR Head, reached number one on Beatport’s Trance (Main Floor) Top 100 New Releases. That chart position is the label’s claim. It is not re-audited here; it is attributed. What the biography is for, in a review of CC004, is scale. This is not a first record by a new producer. It is the first record on this imprint by a producer who already had a label, a radio mix of his own (<em>Riscape Reality Radio</em>, described on the page as a series of the records shaping his sound plus his own), and a conservatory in the résumé. The Club Copy EP is a change of address, not a beginning.`,
      `Four tracks at pop-song length are the formal evidence of the Bandcamp note. “Action,” the longest, is 4:15. Nothing here is a side-long DJ tool. Classic house, the thing the note names, was often both a song and a tool — a vocal record you could also mix. An EP that stays inside five minutes a track is choosing the song. “You Are (Love)” as a title is almost comically direct, which is a house tradition of its own: devotion stated in the plainest words, so the music has to carry the embarrassment or the sincerity. “Make It Right” and “Days Like This” are the same kind of title, the language of repair and ordinary happiness. “Action” is the one that sounds like a function. A sequence that moves from a declaration of love to a demand for action is a small plot. It may be accidental. It is still the plot the titles make.`,
      `The cassette line matters for a label that talks about objects. Digital is the edition anyone can buy. The cassette, sold out, is the edition that can no longer be chosen. Club Copy’s about page describes a record club in which nothing ships unless you choose it. A sold-out tape is the point at which that choice has already been made by someone else. For a first physical edition, that is a clean story: a short run existed, and it went. It is not a story about scarcity as a personality. It is a story about a format doing what small formats do.`,
      `The next Riscape title on the label is <em>Tell Me</em>, CC009, released 10 October 2026, three tracks — “My Whole Life,” “Tell Me Something,” “Tell Me Nothing” — at $4.99. Read back to back with CC004, the titles keep the same habit: plain speech, questions, a life. The critical case for <em>You Are (Love)</em> is that it introduced that habit on Club Copy with the credits in order and the lengths of songs rather than the lengths of sets. The conservatory is context. The four tracks are the work. Days, plural, on the last title. The other spelling can stay on the page that has not been corrected.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/you-are-love`, `You Are (Love)`],
      [`/artists/riscape`, `Riscape`],
      [`/tell-me`, `Tell Me`]
    ]
  },
  "riscape-on-club-copy": {
    format: "Feature",
    kicker: "Feature",
    dek: "Athens, a conservatory, two Club Copy titles, and a chart claim the label bio states and this piece will not pretend to have rechecked.",
    paras: [
      `Riscape is the Club Copy artist whose biography is long enough to check and whose records on the label are few enough to list. The artist page places him in Athens. It says he studied piano at Chalandri’s National Conservatory, learned production at SAE Athens and Grey Studios, and began releasing music in 2020. From 2021 he was on Mystery Freedom, a Dutch imprint, with EPs including <em>Unity</em> and <em>Two x S</em>. The roster line is shorter: “Melodic songwriting meets peak-time energy,” tagged melodic house. The page’s own summary is the same pairing, classical training on one side and clubs on the other. That pairing is a cliché until a record shows which side is load-bearing. On the evidence of the credits, both sides signed the work.`,
      `The chart line has to be handled as a quotation from the bio, not as a trophy this desk weighed. In 2026, the page says, he reached number one on Beatport’s Trance (Main Floor) Top 100 New Releases with a remix of “Synth Magic” by Alphed Le Cornett and JARR Head. Third-party listings of the remix exist; the ranking is the biography’s. A magazine that prints “Beatport number one” as a bare fact, because its own artist page said so, is doing publicity. A magazine that prints “his label bio says he had a Beatport number one” is doing the job. The difference is the whole of editorial independence when the subject pays the hosting.`,
      `What Club Copy itself has released is easier. <em>You Are (Love)</em>, CC004, 18 September 2026: four tracks, 15:02, “You Are (Love),” “Make It Right,” “Action,” and “Days Like This” on the Bandcamp sleeve. Written by Ioannis Rigas, produced by Riscape, for Club Copy and Copy House Publishing. His Bandcamp note asks for vintage and contemporary at once, and for homage to classic house structures. <em>Tell Me</em>, CC009, 10 October 2026: “My Whole Life,” “Tell Me Something,” “Tell Me Nothing,” digital $4.99, with a 24-bit download mentioned on the page. Three weeks between an EP and a second EP is a working pace. It is not a rebrand. The titles on both records speak in ordinary sentences. Love, repair, a kind of day, a whole life, tell me something, tell me nothing. Melodic house, as a market category, often hides that plainness under a longer abstract title. These titles decline the abstraction.`,
      `The conservatory detail is the one that needs translating for a reader who does not care where a producer went to school. Chalandri is a neighborhood in northern Athens. A national conservatory, in this context, means years of piano as a discipline: repertoire, exams, the idea that a note has a right place before it has a mood. SAE and Grey Studios are the later, vocational half — rooms where electronic music is taught as a craft with software and signal flow. Plenty of dance producers have one half of that story. The page insists on both, and then the Club Copy credit splits “written by” from “produced by” under two names for the same person. Ioannis Rigas writes. Riscape produces. The pseudonym is the club name. The legal-looking name is the composing name. That is a small, old-fashioned bit of seriousness, and it matches the Bandcamp note’s talk of homage better than a single brand name would.`,
      `Off the label, the page points to <em>Riscape Reality Radio</em>, a mix series of records he is listening to plus his own, and to support from playlists and DJs it does not list title by title. Support quotes without the supporter’s sentence are not very useful, so they stay in the bio. The useful off-label fact is the mix series, because it rhymes with something this magazine can see elsewhere on the roster. L.T. Drifta’s public work is also split between priced records and a long mixes list. Riscape’s split is between a Dutch label, a trance remix, a radio show, and two Club Copy EPs. A small imprint in British Columbia releasing an Athens producer is not a scene merging. It is a catalogue deciding that melodic house belongs next to Cumberland jungle-and-beats and Cumberland-or-Vancouver house, depending on which Forsberg page you believe. Geography here is the point of the signing, and it does not require a metaphor about distance. The records arrived by the ordinary digital route. The piano study happened in Athens. The catalogue number is CC004 and then CC009.`,
      `Earlier Night Shift copy said the label signed him in 2026, and other copy has said August, and a 2022 interview on this site already speaks of him as if he were around. Those sentences do not agree, so this piece does not pick a signing date. It picks the release dates, which do agree. 18 September 2026, then 10 October 2026. Four songs, then three. A writer’s name and a producer’s name. A chart claim left inside quotation marks. That is a sturdier profile than a packed floor at three in the morning, which no page of ours has documented with a room, a night, or a witness. The music is the part we can point to. The rest can wait for a night that actually gets written down.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/riscape`, `Artist page`],
      [`/you-are-love`, `You Are (Love)`],
      [`/news/please`, `The EP note`]
    ]
  },
  "beat-tape": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "A 22 August 2023 note announced a CJ Archer beat tape. The current Club Copy catalogue does not list one.",
    paras: [
      `On 22 August 2023 this desk published a short note called “Beat Tape” and aimed it at CJ Archer, the producer the artist page now describes as based in Vancouver and as the founder of Club Copy. The note said the tape was short instrumentals, that nothing on it was begging to be a single, and that it was the place to hear the label before the later records got “prettier.” It linked to the artist page and the library. It did not link to a release page, because there wasn’t a release page to link. There still isn’t. The catalogue, as the site publishes it, runs from L.T. Drifta, Forsberg, and Riscape. No CJ Archer title appears among the albums, EPs, and singles for sale.`,
      `That absence is the story, and it is a better story than the atmosphere the note was selling. Beat tapes are a specific object in hip-hop. They are how producers circulate beats that are not yet songs: a sequence of instrumentals, often unfinished on purpose, often given away or sold cheaply, so a rapper or a listener can hear the maker’s hand without a single’s campaign. Stones Throw built a public around that object. So did a generation of producers who treated the DAT, the CD-R, and later the private link as the real discography, with the official album as a selection from it. When a label’s own writer announces a beat tape and the label’s shop never lists it, one of three things has happened. The tape was a plan. The tape existed and was withdrawn. Or the tape was a metaphor for a folder of loops, and the metaphor got a headline.`,
      `The 2023 note leans on the third reading and then talks as if the object were real. “Before the catalogue had a spine, there was a folder.” A folder is not a tape. A tape, even a digital one, has a sequence and a title and usually a date you can cite. This magazine cannot cite a tracklist it was never given, and it will not describe drums it cannot point a reader toward. What it can cite is the rest of CJ Archer’s public credit on the site. The about page says Club Copy was founded by Charlie Archer (CJ Archer) on the data’s founding date of 12 February 2024 — which is after this note. The <em>j'adore</em> page, released 10 August 2026, lists him as producer of Forsberg’s two-track single. The artist page offers a portrait-sized biography of one line: producer, Vancouver. Between a pre-founding note about a tape and a 2026 production credit on someone else’s single, the verifiable discography of the editor-as-producer is that credit. One single. Named. On a page anyone can open.`,
      `There is a critical habit, common to small imprints and to zines that share a staff with them, of treating the unreleased folder as the authentic work and the released record as the compromise. The habit feels romantic and it fails a simple test. Authenticity that cannot be played for a stranger is a private experience. It can be true and still be useless to an archive. Night Shift’s job, if the vision on the wall means anything, is the archive: pieces that are still worth reading when the mood has expired. A beat tape that cannot be found is not an archive. It is a gesture. The honest gesture, in 2026, is to say the August 2023 piece overclaimed, and to point at the credit that does exist.`,
      `<em>j'adore</em> is not a beat tape. It is a finished house single, two songs, produced by the person the tape note was about, released under another artist’s name. That may be what “the later records got prettier” was trying, clumsily, to predict. Pretty is the wrong word for a production credit. Specific is the right one. Drums someone else performs inside, loops that end, a title, a price, a date. If a CJ Archer instrumental tape surfaces with a tracklist and a page, it can have a review of its own, and this essay can be a footnote to it. Until then, the footnote is the essay. The founder’s audible contribution to the catalogue, on the pages we have, is the production of CC007. Start there. The folder can stay a folder.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/cj-archer`, `CJ Archer`],
      [`/j-adore`, `j'adore`],
      [`/library`, `Library`]
    ]
  },
  "lions-gate": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "A 1 June 2024 note named a L.T. Drifta cut after the bridge. The current artist page does not list the title.",
    paras: [
      `Lions Gate Bridge is a real bridge. It crosses Burrard Inlet into Vancouver from the north, and it is one of the ordinary ways the city continues after dark. On 1 June 2024 Night Shift published a note that treated the name as a L.T. Drifta track: a cut for the moment you meant to go home and did not, “water under,” email unfinished, the night still open. The note linked to his artist page and to the signing piece. It did not link to a release, and the site does not have a Lions’ Gate release page now.`,
      `The artist page, as it stands, is explicit about scope. Nine titles. <em>Lavery - Shaolin Style (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em>, <em>The Chameleon - Links (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em>, <em>Omni Trio - Renegade Snares (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em>, <em>Nia Archives - Get Me Down (Molly's Hazy Jungle Edit)</em>, <em>Three 4 Zone</em>, <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em>, <em>Summer Madness</em>, <em>Classic Jazz</em>, <em>Champion Sound</em>. Catalogue numbers CC028, CC024, CC023, CC020, CC014, CC008, CC006, CC003, CC001. No <em>Lions’ Gate</em>. A zine that keeps a review of a record its own label no longer lists has two honest options. It can delete the memory, which is how archives get tidy and false. Or it can say what the page said, date it, and say what the artist page says now. This is the second option. The 2024 note is a document of an intention or of a title that circulated inside the desk. It is not a document of a record you can buy.`,
      `The bridge, stripped of the track we cannot verify, is still a useful piece of local geography for a label that keeps tripping over its own place-names. Cumberland, where Club Copy’s Bandcamp says the label lives and where L.T. Drifta’s page says he lives, is on Vancouver Island. Vancouver is across the Strait of Georgia. Lions Gate is a Vancouver bridge, not an Island one. Naming a piece of music after it, in a note published from a label with an Island address, is a way of pointing at the city as the place the night goes, not as the place the artist is from. That reading was available in 2024 and it did not require the track to be mystical. It required the track to exist. Without the track, the reading is about the note, not about the music. The desk wanted a local image with movement in it, and it reached for the bridge commuters already use as a shorthand for staying out.`,
      `There is a pattern here, and <a href="/news/any-jungle">any jungle</a> is the other half of it. Two L.T. Drifta titles got Night Shift reviews and then did not appear among the nine titles the artist page now calls the catalogue. A third absence is the beat-tape note attached to CJ Archer. Small labels revise. They pull records, they rename projects, they decide a white-label experiment is not part of the story they want a new listener to enter through. Revision is allowed. Pretending the earlier page was never published is not, because the URL still resolves. Anyone who bookmarked the June 2024 piece can still land on it. The correction belongs on the piece.`,
      `What remains worth saying about Lions Gate, the bridge, in a music magazine is modest. Cities keep a few pieces of infrastructure that double as emotional punctuation. A bridge at night is one of them, in Vancouver as in any city that has to be crossed to get to the late room or away from it. Electronic music has named itself after roads, lines, and junctions for decades because the music is often about duration: how long you are willing to stay in transit. A label based on an island, releasing music that its own album copy sometimes sets in “Vancouver late hours,” is going to keep feeling that crossing. The feeling is not a single. The single, if it was one, is not in the nine.`,
      `Readers who want L.T. Drifta’s Club Copy music have a better door. <em>Lavery - Shaolin Style (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em> on 24 October 2026. <em>The Chameleon - Links (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em> on 23 October 2026. <em>Omni Trio - Renegade Snares (L.T.'s VIP Mix)</em> on 23 October 2026. <em>Get Me Down</em> on 19 October 2026. <em>Champion Sound</em> on 9 July 2024 by the Bandcamp date. <em>Classic Jazz</em> and <em>Summer Madness</em> after it. <em>Lakeshore West 23:55</em> on the site and not on his Bandcamp. <em>Three 4 Zone</em> on 18 October 2026. Those are the records. The bridge can go back to being a bridge. The note can go back to being a note from the first summer of the magazine, when the desk was faster than the catalogue and the catalogue, sensibly, did not follow every title the desk got excited about.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/lt-drifta`, `L.T. Drifta`],
      [`/news/any-jungle`, `any jungle`]
    ]
  },
  "any-jungle": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "The 30 August 2026 note reviewed a L.T. Drifta title the artist page's nine-record list does not include.",
    paras: [
      `On 30 August 2026 Night Shift ran a review of a L.T. Drifta record called <em>any jungle</em>. The page described a long piece, a white-vinyl image with Club Copy lettering, and a kind of jungle that “doesn’t treat you like a tempo problem.” The artwork file is still on the site. A ninety-second preview file is in the site’s audio folders, which is the length of a store preview, not evidence of the full duration. What is not on the site is a release page, a price, a catalogue number, or a line on the artist page. That page says nine titles. <em>any jungle</em> is not one of them.`,
      `This is the same situation as <a href="/news/lions-gate">Lions’ Gate</a>, and it wants the same remedy. The URL of the review remains. The claim that the record is part of the catalogue does not. Keeping the old review’s sensory writing — lashes, coats, a clock the writer forgot to look at — would be keeping a listening diary of an object the label is no longer offering. Diaries can be true and still be the wrong text to leave in front of a new reader. The right text is a short accounting.`,
      `Jungle, as a word, is otherwise all over L.T. Drifta’s presentation and almost nowhere on his release genres. The artist page tags him “Jungle” and “the soulful side of jungle and hip-hop.” The roster says “Jungle / Breaks.” The nine priced records are instrumental hip-hop, jazz, and electronic. So a title as blunt as <em>any jungle</em> would have been, if it had stayed in the list, the one object that used the genre as a name rather than as a bio. Its absence means the genre word is still a bio word. The records you can pay for do not put it on the sticker. The mixes come closer. On the mixes page, several uploads are titled <em>Deep in Tha Jungle</em> and dated, in the titles, to the mid-1990s: 1993–96, 1994 hardcore, 1996 atmosphere. Those are mixes, attributed on the page to L.T. Drifta, not originals for sale. They are also the place where the word “jungle” is doing documentary work — pointing at a period of records — rather than promotional work.`,
      `A ninety-second preview without a release is a particular kind of digital leftover. Stores use short previews so you can sample a track you might buy. A preview that outlives the product page is a clip with nowhere to land. Describing the music inside it, in any detail, would be reviewing a sample of an unpublished or withdrawn record. That can be done when an artist asks for it. It should not be done by a label’s magazine in order to fill an old URL. The image alt on the original story — white vinyl, black ground, Club Copy lettering — is a description of a picture, and the picture can stay. Pictures of sleeves for records that did not remain on the list are part of how labels look while they are deciding. They are not the discography.`,
      `The discography a stranger should trust is the one the artist page is willing to count. Nine. Plus, if they want the listening, forty mixes on the mixes page, including a series called <em>NIGHTSHIFT</em> that this issue writes about separately. <em>any jungle</em> belongs with the drafts, the pulled experiments, and the titles that made sense in a month and less sense in the binder. August 2026 was a busy month on this desk. The note got a headline, a dek, and a mood. The catalogue, by October, had not adopted it. Catalogues are allowed the last word. They are the part a listener can hold the label to.`,
      `If the title returns, with a number and a date and a full file, it can be reviewed as music. Until then the sentence is administrative and, for that reason, more durable than the one it replaces. <em>any jungle</em> was written up here on 30 August 2026. It is not among the nine titles L.T. Drifta’s Club Copy page lists. The jungle in his public work is in the bio and in the mix titles. The records are elsewhere, and they have numbers.`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/lt-drifta`, `L.T. Drifta`],
      [`/tapes`, `Mixtapes`]
    ]
  },
  "cj-archer-interview": {
    format: "Interview",
    kicker: "Interview",
    dek: "The 27 October 2022 conversation, kept as printed, and read against the catalogue the label actually released.",
    paras: [
      `This is the only interview Night Shift has published, and the subject is the editor. On 27 October 2022 CJ Archer answered five questions about Vancouver, the size of the label, the roster, the word “underground,” and what a new listener should play. The about page now says Club Copy was founded by Charlie Archer (CJ Archer). The <em>j'adore</em> sleeve credits him as producer. So the interview is not a visit from outside. It is the house talking to itself, early, before most of the records that now define the catalogue existed. The answers below are the ones printed then. They have not been improved. After them is the part 2022 could not know: what the pages say in 2026, and where the answers and the pages diverge.`,
      `There’s a certain kind of music that takes a while to find its way out. CJ Archer has spent years around it: records, half-finished projects, old machines, the Pacific Northwest underground that never needed a coastal magazine to exist. The conversation was about Club Copy, the weather, and why a finished record is usually a feeling somebody refused to throw away.`
    ],
    afterParas: [
      `<p class="news-q">You started this from Vancouver. What does that actually sound like, besides rain?</p>`,
      `<p>It sounds like rooms that aren’t trying to be Brooklyn. Wet streets. A shop you can stand in too long. Ferries. The kind of night where the show is thirty people and somebody brought a cake. I like music that can live in that. If it only works in a caption, I’m not interested.</p>`,
      `<p class="news-q">Club Copy is tiny on purpose. Is that a romance or a strategy?</p>`,
      `<p>Both, if I’m honest. Small editions mean you still know who might hold the thing. Catalogue numbers. A cassette that has to be dubbed. I’m not allergic to a bigger room. I just don’t want the music to start performing for one. The record club is the invite: first listens, a discount if you’re in, nothing shipped unless you choose it. That’s enough infrastructure for me.</p>`,
      `<p class="news-q">Molly Haze, Forsberg, L.T. Drifta, Riscape: how do you know when someone’s ours?</p>`,
      `<p>I know when I want to leave it on. Not when I can explain it. Molly’s from Cumberland, house that feels like a private temperature. Forsberg’s Vancouver locked-drums, late-car energy. Drifta digs like it’s a practice. Riscape came in from Athens with piano in his hands and peak-time in his chest. None of them asked to match. They just all sound like they were finished by a person, not a moodboard.</p>`,
      `<p class="news-q">What’s the part people get wrong about “underground”?</p>`,
      `<p>That it’s a costume. Or a purity test. Underground is just the work you do when nobody’s coming. Then if people come, you try not to sand off the thing that made you stay. Gossip, rooms, lyrics on trial, a club selling tickets and still saying no at the door: that’s all the same story. Who gets to keep the feeling, and who tries to checkout-button it.</p>`,
      `<p class="news-q">Last one. What should somebody play tonight, if they’ve never been here?</p>`,
      `<p><em>j'adore</em> if they want to be in a car. The Drifta mixtape if they want the house to keep going after they think they’re done. And if they want the long memory: <em>Donuts</em>, then whatever we put out that still has dust on it.</p>`,
      `<p>Read against the catalogue, the 2022 answers are partly prophecy and partly a map that went stale in specific places. The ferry sentence holds. Cumberland and Vancouver are separated by water, and the label’s Bandcamp now says Cumberland while several artist pages say Vancouver. Molly Haze’s page does put her in Cumberland, and the roster calls the music “UK garage made for sweaty dancefloors and staying out all night,” with a one-word bio, “k-i-s-s-i-n-g.” No Molly Haze release is listed. The “private temperature” line is the interview’s, not a discography. Forsberg “from Vancouver” is the sentence the label still prints and the sentence his Bandcamp contradicts: “House music from Cumberland, BC.” Locked drums and long loops survived; they are the artist-page copy in 2026. The city did not survive as a single fact. L.T. Drifta “digs like it’s a practice” is the answer that aged into something checkable. Nine titles, plus forty mixes on the mixes page, is a practice. Riscape from Athens with piano is exactly what his page says, conservatory included, and the peak-time claim is the roster’s. He has two Club Copy EPs, not a myth.</p>`,
      `<p>The last answer names <em>j'adore</em> and a Drifta mixtape and <em>Donuts</em>. <em>j'adore</em> would not be released until 10 August 2026, and when it arrived the producer credit was CJ Archer’s. Recommending it in 2022 means he was recommending work that was not yet a public Club Copy record, and recommending a record he would later be credited with producing. That is allowed in a conversation. It is a poor “start here” now, unless the reader wants the credit as part of the story. The Drifta mixes do exist, in public, on the mixes page. <em>Donuts</em> is the one recommendation that was already a finished, famous record in 2022 and is still the same record. J Dilla’s album does not depend on our catalogue. It is the stable object in an answer otherwise made of futures.</p>`,
      `<p>Tiny on purpose is the answer the shop can test. Catalogue numbers are real: the releases carry CC codes. A cassette is real at least once — <em>You Are (Love)</em>, sold out, described on its page as the first Club Copy cassette. “Nothing shipped unless you choose it” matches the about page’s account of the record club. The strategy half of the answer is visible. The romance half is the interview itself: five questions, no outside voice, a subject who is the publisher. Underground, in his definition here, is work done when nobody is coming. A self-interview is a complicated example. Nobody from outside came. The work that holds up is the part a stranger can still put on: the numbered records, the mixes, the cassette that already sold through. The feeling he refused to throw away is not on the page. The records are.</p>`
    ],
    ctas: [
      [`/artists/cj-archer`, `CJ Archer`],
      [`/about`, `About the label`],
      [`/j-adore`, `j'adore`]
    ]
  }
};
